theory.intro:
Many practical quantities are areas under a graph. If a graph gives a **rate of change**, the area under it from $t=a$ to $t=b$ is the total change over that interval. The unit of the answer comes from multiplying the units on the two axes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!20] (1,0) -- plot[domain=1:5,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{1.2+0.5*\x-0.05*\x*\x}) -- (5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (6.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.1);
\draw[thick,domain=0.2:6,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{1.2+0.5*\x-0.05*\x*\x});
\draw (1,0.1) -- (1,-0.1) node[below] {$a$};
\draw (5,0.1) -- (5,-0.1) node[below] {$b$};
\node at (2.9,1.0) {total change};
\node at (4.7,3.5) {$y=R(t)$};
\node[below] at (3.0,-0.9) {Time};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.0,1.9) {Rate};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,1.2) -- (4.25,3.0) -- (4.25,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.4,0);
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,3.8);
\draw[thick] (0,1.2) -- (4.25,3.0);
\foreach \t in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw ({0.85*\t},0.1) -- ({0.85*\t},-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$\t$};
\foreach \p/\lab in {0.6/10,1.2/20,1.8/30,2.4/40,3.0/50} \draw (0.1,\p) -- (-0.1,\p) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$\lab$};
\node[below] at (2.1,-0.85) {Time $t$ (minutes)};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,1.7) {Rate $R$ (litres/min)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A tank initially contains $100$ litres of water. Water flows into it at a rate of $R=20+6t$ litres per minute, for $0\le t\le 5$ minutes.
Find the volume of water in the tank after $5$ minutes.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:3,smooth,variable=\x] ({1.3*\x},{0.1*(3*\x*\x+4)}) -- (3.9,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.0,0);
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,3.8);
\draw[thick,domain=0:3,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({1.3*\x},{0.1*(3*\x*\x+4)});
\foreach \t in {1,2,3} \draw ({1.3*\t},0.1) -- ({1.3*\t},-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$\t$};
\foreach \p/\lab in {1/10,2/20,3/30} \draw (0.1,\p) -- (-0.1,\p) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$\lab$};
\node[below] at (1.95,-0.85) {Time $t$ (hours)};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,1.7) {Rate $R$ (litres/hour)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A generator's fuel tank initially contains $80$ litres of fuel. The generator burns fuel at a rate of $R=3t^{2}+4$ litres per hour, for $0\le t\le 3$ hours.
Find the volume of fuel remaining in the tank after $3$ hours.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-2.1,0) -- plot[domain=-3:3,smooth,variable=\x] ({0.7*\x},{0.3*(9-\x*\x)}) -- (2.1,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-3.1,0) -- (3.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-3:3,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({0.7*\x},{0.3*(9-\x*\x)});
\draw (-2.1,0.1) -- (-2.1,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$-3$};
\draw (2.1,0.1) -- (2.1,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$3$};
\draw (0.1,2.7) -- (-0.1,2.7) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$9$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.85) {$x$ (metres)};
\node at (2.5,3.2) {$y=9-x^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The cross-section of a tunnel is the region between the curve $y=9-x^{2}$ and the level ground $y=0$, with $x$ and $y$ measured in metres.
Find the area of the cross-section.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:4,smooth,variable=\x] ({0.9*\x},{0.6*(4*\x-\x*\x)}) -- (3.6,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.6,0) -- (4.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0:4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({0.9*\x},{0.6*(4*\x-\x*\x)});
\draw (1.8,0.1) -- (1.8,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$2$};
\draw (3.6,0.1) -- (3.6,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$4$};
\draw (0.1,2.4) -- (-0.1,2.4) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$4$};
\node[below] at (1.8,-0.85) {$x$ (metres)};
\node at (3.6,3.0) {$y=4x-x^{2}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A garden bed is bounded by the curve $y=4x-x^{2}$ and the straight path along $y=0$, with $x$ and $y$ measured in metres.
Find the area of the garden bed.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-2.2,0) -- plot[domain=-4:4,smooth,variable=\x] ({0.55*\x},{0.45*(0.25*\x*\x-4)}) -- (2.2,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-3.2,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,1.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-4:4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({0.55*\x},{0.45*(0.25*\x*\x-4)});
\draw (-2.2,0.1) -- (-2.2,-0.1) node[above,font=\footnotesize] {$-4$};
\draw (2.2,0.1) -- (2.2,-0.1) node[above,font=\footnotesize] {$4$};
\draw (0.1,-1.8) -- (-0.1,-1.8) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$-4$};
\node[right,font=\small] at (2.4,0.35) {ground level};
\node at (-2.4,-2.2) {$y=\frac{x^{2}}{4}-4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A drainage channel is cut into level ground.
Its cross-section is bounded above by the ground line $y=0$ and below by the curve $y=\frac{x^{2}}{4}-4$, with $x$ and $y$ in metres.
Find the area of the cross-section.

development.question_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- (0,2.4) -- (2.55,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (2.55,0) -- (4.25,-1.6) -- (4.25,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.5,0) -- (5.2,0);
\draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,3.2);
\draw[thick] (0,2.4) -- (4.25,-1.6);
\foreach \t in {1,2,3,4,5} \draw ({0.85*\t},0.08) -- ({0.85*\t},-0.08);
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (2.55,0.15) {$3$};
\node[above,font=\footnotesize] at (4.25,0.15) {$5$};
\foreach \p/\lab in {-1.6/-4,-0.8/-2,0.8/2,1.6/4,2.4/6} \draw (0.08,\p) -- (-0.08,\p) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$\lab$};
\node[below] at (2.1,-2.5) {Time $t$ (seconds)};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,0.5) {Rate $R$ (litres/s)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A tank initially contains $50$ litres of water. Water enters it at a rate of $R=6-2t$ litres per second, for $0\le t\le 5$ seconds.
A negative rate means water is leaving.
Find the volume of water in the tank after $5$ seconds.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:4,smooth,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.8*exp(-0.5*\x)}) -- (4,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.1,0);
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,3.6);
\draw[thick,domain=0:4,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.8*exp(-0.5*\x)});
\foreach \t in {1,2,3,4} \draw (\t,0.1) -- (\t,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$\t$};
\foreach \p/\lab in {0.7/10,1.4/20,2.1/30,2.8/40} \draw (0.1,\p) -- (-0.1,\p) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$\lab$};
\node[below] at (2.0,-0.85) {Time $t$ (minutes)};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,1.6) {Rate $R$ (litres/min)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A leaking drum initially contains $200$ litres of liquid. It loses liquid at a rate of $R=40e^{-0.5t}$ litres per minute, for $0\le t\le 4$ minutes.
Find the volume remaining after $4$ minutes, correct to one decimal place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0,0) -- plot[domain=0:9,smooth,variable=\x] ({0.45*\x},{3/(\x+1)}) -- (4.05,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.1,0);
\draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,3.8);
\draw[thick,domain=0:9,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({0.45*\x},{3/(\x+1)});
\foreach \t in {3,6,9} \draw ({0.45*\t},0.1) -- ({0.45*\t},-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$\t$};
\foreach \p/\lab in {0.6/10,1.2/20,1.8/30,2.4/40,3.0/50} \draw (0.1,\p) -- (-0.1,\p) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$\lab$};
\node[below] at (2.0,-0.85) {Time $t$ (minutes)};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.4,1.7) {Rate $R$ (litres/min)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A tank initially contains $300$ litres of water. Water flows into it at a rate of $R=\frac{50}{t+1}$ litres per minute, for $0\le t\le 9$ minutes.
Find the volume of water in the tank after $9$ minutes, correct to one decimal place.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (-3.1416,0) -- plot[domain=-1.5708:1.5708,smooth,variable=\x] ({2*\x},{2.8*cos(deg(\x))}) -- (3.1416,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-4.0,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5) -- (0,3.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=-1.5708:1.5708,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({2*\x},{2.8*cos(deg(\x))});
\draw (-3.1416,0.1) -- (-3.1416,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$-\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (3.1416,0.1) -- (3.1416,-0.1) node[below,font=\footnotesize] {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.1,2.8) -- (-0.1,2.8) node[left,font=\footnotesize] {$2$};
\node[below] at (0,-1.1) {$x$ (metres)};
\node at (3.0,3.1) {$y=2\cos x$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
A curved window is bounded by the curve $y=2\cos x$ and its horizontal sill $y=0$, for $-\frac{\pi}{2}\le x\le\frac{\pi}{2}$, with $x$ and $y$ in metres.
Find the area of glass in the window.
